Taylor Swift Remembers Dolly Parton as &#8216;True Grace & True Grit, All in One Dazzling&nbsp;Person&#8217;

Beating out Ariana Grande, Madonna, Bruno Mars, GENER8ION and Sabrina Carpenter, Swift accepted the Moon Person from host Snoop Dogg and began her remarks by thanking her fans for voting. &#8220;I&#8217;m so proud of it,&#8221; she said of the self-directed visual, which dropped in October 2025. &#8220;I wanted it to seem like one continuous shot, so I learned a lot about hiding cuts in places.&#8221;

The 14-time Grammy winner continued on to shout out her collaborators on the project, namely cinematographer Rodrigo Prieto, whom Swift said &#8220;gave [her] the confidence&#8221; to start directing her own music videos.

&#8220;Lastly and most importantly, I want to thank and dedicate this award to one person without whom this wouldn&#8217;t be happening,&#8221; she said. &#8220;That person is the ultimate showgirl, Dolly Parton. Every single song that she wrote &#8230; her storytelling was so vivid and rich, listening to her songs, it was like watching the best music video you&#8217;ve ever seen in your life, every single time.&#8221;

Parton died at 80 years old the end of August following a cancer battle, sending shockwaves across culture. At the time, Swift released a statement praising &#8220;the melodies and lyrics [Dolly] wrote that thawed even the coldest hearts,&#8221; and at this year&#8217;s VMAs, the Eras headliner also acknowledged how the Queen of Country poured so much love and gratitude into her fans.

&#8220;I am lucky I got to be one of them, and I will always be one of them,&#8221; Swift added. &#8220;I love you all so very much, and I think that we were all very lucky to share this planet with Dolly Parton for a time. Thank you for this amazing moment. I love you all.&#8221;

Swift&#8217;s big win came after an already eventful night for the pop star, who also accepted the first-ever artist director award and premiered her new &#8220;Patient Zero&#8221; music video during the award show. She went into the night as the second-most nominated artist of 2026 with 11 nods, behind only Madonna, who had 13.

Plus, the singer&#8217;s victory extends her record for most video of the year wins in VMAs history. She previously won the category for &#8220;Bad Blood&#8221; (2015), &#8220;You Need to Calm Down&#8221; (2019), &#8220;All Too Well: The Short Film&#8221; (2022), &#8220;Anti-Hero&#8221; (2023) and &#8220;Fortnight&#8221; (2024). The video for &#8220;The Fate of Ophelia&#8221; &#8212; which spent 10 weeks atop the Billboard Hot 100 , the singer&#8217;s longest running No. 1 hit on the chart &#8212; becomes her sixth to win.

Elsewhere in the program, Kacey Musgraves delivered an emotional tribute to Parton, harmonizing with an old video of the late icon singing &#8220;I Will Always Love You.&#8221; Madonna opened the show with a medley of Confessions II songs &#8212; joined by Sabrina Carpenter, Charli xcx and more celebrity guests &#8212; while RAYE, Sienna Spiro, LISA and others performed throughout the night as well.
